                        Section 0 - Introduction

------------------------------------------------------------------------


0.0 Overview:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

I must thank Ben Margolin for the inspiration for this door.  His
'Masterfile BBS List' has been used for some time now on my system,
although I was never real happy how an Australian telephone number could
not be entered into it.

Besides, I'm losing faith in AC-BASIC doors, if my system is ever going
to crash these days, then 9 times out of 10, it's an AC- door.  In fact
my AmyInfo is the worst culprit (it allows the users to get down too low
among the OS).  The way AC handles its memory leaves a bit to be
desired, because for starters, if an AC (or even Hi-Soft) door is quit,
the custom colour palette is stripped from the front-most screen at the
time.  Happens with every Hi-Soft and AC door.  They strip palettes from
programs as diverse as Cygnus Ed, ProWrite and Quarterback.  (Not to
mention Metro - but it usually closes its screen down when a door is
running, so it's safe!)

But what I really was trying to do with this door was experiment.  I
haven't actually written a door for a month or so now, I've been
concentrating on Metro itself, but I got sick of the old serial
routines, and decided to re-write them for speed, and ease of use.  I
also wanted to take a better look at the Metro editor.  So here we have
it.  A new door that uses my new serial routines, and a re-vamped
version of the old editor.  AND guess what?  They work!

They are also a lot easier to use.  Instead of going:

o$="Text to print"
mod.out%=1
in%=1
il%=25
GOSUB 1

I can simply go:

msend("Text to Print")
mget(1,25,0,1)

You'll notice a lot of other experimental features in this door.  I'll
explain later....

[This is the last door I wrote before Plutonic, incidentally, you can
see the evolution from the power of the doors.  This one's got HEAPS!]

------------------------------------------------------------------------


0.1 Changes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~

Version 1.7 (28-Nov-94)

Changed to not use Metro.config, but Metro.cfg instead, or none at all
if not available.  Changed a few dates, and the node numbers and phone
numbers for Inquestor in the Help and other displays.  Didn't write the
search routine, will do if encouraged by a few $$$ from people who want
this sort of support (hint, hint).

This door will run off TransAmiga, incidentally, see the section below
for how to do it - pretty easy, really!


Version 1.6 (04-Apr-92)

A few cosmetic changes. A general version registered to 'ALL', without
the restrictions on the database size, but no search facility yet.

Mainly released in this form to be run as a TransAmiga door. Users of
Skyline and any other BBS that uses Arexx can easily use this method to
run Metro Doors. More will be coming, this is probably the best example
to release, as it's not Metro-specific.

The code for this door is becoming a dim memory now :-) I daren't modify
it too much because it was working pretty bloody well, if I remember
rightly!


Version 1.5 (28-Nov-91)

The modem is opened in shared access mode. I must thank Percy and Alex
for writing the shared modem routines, something I omitted to do with
the InqMail distribution. Sorry guys, I just had no idea where the code
came from...

(Semi-) swatted a bug. In version 1.2 if you deleted an entry from the
database it would work fine. However if you then tried to delete a
second entry without quitting back to the main menu and returning to the
sysop maint section, it would crash (it thought there was one more entry
in the database than you actually had).

Now it will quit you back from the sysop maintenance menu after each
database deletion. I know it's not perfect, but the main reason for this
update was the shared modem access, and I don't have enough time to
spend on this for the time being. It was pretty quick to fix this
problem up by adopting this method.

There are a few calls to purge the serial port now, when menus are being
printed. This saves the menu repeating a few times if the users grow
impatient and hit keys before the door is ready.

My new standard door screen, borderless main window and status window
are in place.  This is nice and clean and gives you quite a few lines of
display, even on an NTSC system.

You can now run the door in what's referred to as iconised mode.
Pressing F3 while the door is waiting for input will stop local screen
output, and pressing F3 again turns it back on.

The Search database option is NOT written, even in the registered
version. Sorry - again time is killing me on this score. It will come
out later - probably January 1992 or so, I imagine. (Oh well.....)



------------------------------------------------------------------------


0.2 Description:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

BBBase is an online database program for storing details of BBS systems.
It has full ANSI graphics, is VERY easy to setup, and has the most
powerful sysop maintenance section of any Metro Door in existence.

All the sysop functions are available from remote and include the
ability to edit, rename, backup, delete, copy and list the files inside
the door's directory.  It also allows the sysop to edit any part of the
database, or delete entries from it.

User messages to the sysop are supported, as well as the ability to
delegate maintenance authority to users of a certain security through a
config file, if so desired.

The database is reasonably extensive and includes fields for:

BBS Name
Sysop
Phone number (including area code)
BBS Software in use
Baud Rates supported
Number of lines
Original poster of the entry
Date the record was last modified
A comment line - which may contain anything

Also, a textfile with detailed information about the BBS is supported.
If the poster of the entry sees fit, he/she may include a free-form text
of up to 250 lines about the BBS.

The door contains online help about each command.  Listing of the
database can be done in two forms:  a verbose listing with one screen
per BBS, or a terse form summary with one line per BBS.

Safe error-checking routines are in place, and the carrier-drop function
is going to be the fastest of any Metro door, simply because we check
the carrier on both modem receives AND sends.  This is faster than Metro
itself, in fact, as Metro only checks while the system is waiting for
input, not sending it out.

The door weighs in at well over 3,000 lines of code, indicative of just
how much there actually is in it.

Extended textfile commands are in place when the BBS is outputting text.
A user can press the spacebar or 'S' to abort, Ctrl-S to pause and any
other key to restart.  This applies to every text output including the
Sysop Maintenance section, so don't forget about it, eh?

Oh, and for local logins on an NTSC system, you get 22 lines displayed
on the screen.  Set your page pausing length to 21 lines - it will
default to 23 otherwise.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


0.3 StandAlone Operation:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Version 1.7 WILL run in standalone mode even without a Metro.cfg.
Simply CD into the door's directory and run the door.exe file - easy!
Without an s:Metro.cfg file, an NTSC screen will be used.

If you're running standalone and want a PAL screen, make a file called
s:Metro.cfg and include in it these four lines:

BBSName Anything you like
SysopName Anything you like
WindowWidth 640
WindowHeight 256

(you don't really have to have BBSName and SysopName, but if you're
making up this file anyway, why not?)

NB: OzMetro sysops will already have one of these files!!!


