                           Section 1 - Setup

------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.0 Copy Command in C:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

BBBase needs only 1 extra file to run.  And that is, a copy of the DOS
'Copy' command in the c:  directory.  I think most of you will have this
file in the correct place already.  The COPY command is only used with
the Sysop Function 'Backup A File' so even without it you'll still be
able to use 95% of the features of the door.  Problem is, I think Metro
itself uses it, as well as a few of my other doors, too...


------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.1 Config File:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

BBBase has a small three-line config file called 'BBBase.cfg'.  It is
completely optional, and if a config file cannot be found, or is not
valid, then it will simply revert to the internal defaults.

A config file may look like this:

7
27
128


Not very impressive, eh?  Here's what the three numbers mean:


Line 1 is the level of sysop access for this door.  If you wish to
delegate authority for managing the database, this is the minimum level
required to access the sysop functions.  Beware!  The sysop functions
are VERY POWERFUL, so ensure your users of this level can be trusted not
to go deleting files left right and centre!  Without the config, this
value will default to level 8 (not level 9 as you may expect).  So if
you only want level 9 users to have access to the door, you MUST make up
a config.

(You could even set it to greater than 9 if you wanted, meaning NOBODY
AT ALL could get to the sysop functions - maybe you'd have a reason for
this, but I can't think of one - yes I can:  your sysop password has
been compromised and you don't want remote callers to access the door in
case they get nasty and delete all the data files in the door
directory).

Line 2 is the lowest ASCII value able to be entered into the door's
editor.  It defaults to 27.  If you don't want users to be able to enter
ANSI in the messages and texts, set it to 28.  You must ensure this
value is AT MOST 32, otherwise certain characters will not be able to be
entered into the editor!!  It also must be at least 20, to lock out some
of the more exotic control characters.  If the loascii value is not in
the range 20-32 inclusive, the door will use the internal defaults for
ALL config values.

The last line is the highest ASCII character you can enter into the
editor.  It defaults to 255.  However you can lock out high ASCII chars
by setting it to a lower value.  The MINIMUM value allowable is 123.  If
you set a value below 123, your config will be ignored and the door will
revert to the internal defaults.  The maximum is somewhere around
2,100,000,000, but that's irrelevant.  A user cannot send an ASCII code
greater than 255 at you through a modem!

After the third line, I recommend you press your return key about 4 or 5
times, to ensure the End Of File bogey of GFA BASIC is well and truly
averted.

The config is really only there because I don't like hard-coding stuff
into programs, preferring to let the user make the decision.  However,
from what I see, users don't like making config files.  So for this
door, if the config confuses you, forget all about it.  Just make sure
that there is no 'BBBase.cfg' in the door directory, and it will use the
defaults.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.2 Files created:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In the door directory, a number of files will be created by the door:

BBBase.dat            The database itself.
SysopMessages         Messages to the sysop from users.
BBSName.txt           A textfile with up to 250 lines of info about
                      a BBS listed in the database.

For this reason, do not keep a file in the door directory with these
names, as the door will overwrite them.  Also any file with the
extension #?.txt has a very small chance of being overwritten.  So don't
keep any '.txt' files in here, either!


------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.3 Error Checking:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This door has error-checking, and should anything at all go wrong, it
will simply quit back to the BBS.  Even if it gets an End-Of-File!  Use
it with confidence!!

If an error exception is trapped, the door will write out to a file
called 'error.log' in the door directory, briefly listing the time and
date of the error, who was online at the time, and a brief description
of the actual error that occured.

The only errors I've gotten from this door have been from the Sysop
Maintenance section whilst (a) trying to delete a directory, not a file,
and (b) renaming a file to a name across to another device (EG from DH0:
to DF1:  - AmigaDOS doesn't allow this!).  Besides, I was actually
trying to CAUSE errors when I got them!

However, I wrote the program, so I am probably going to be treating it
more kindly than you.  So if you should ever notice an 'error.log'
created in the door's directory, please have a look at it, and if you
like, ask me about it.  Please try to ask the online user a brief
run-down of what happened, though.  If you can't reproduce bugs, you
can't fix them.

Some errors will immediately be apparent - such as "File Not Found" or
"End Of File", indicating your setup needs inspection.  In the case of
an End Of File, it would be a good idea to get Percy's FileRead or my
Muddles program out and check all the textfiles in the directory.

Really, this program is very robust, and I doubt you'll ever get an
error.  Famous last words, these ones.

------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.4 Metro Installation Example
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Find yourself a vacant door number to use in a DOORFILESx directory.
Let's assume you are going to set this up the same way I have it:  Door
number 210 in Doorfiles1/.

Make a directory called 'DOOR210' in Doorfiles1/, and for now, just copy
all the files from the archive into this directory.  The only file you
actually need is the executable itself!  I strongly request you leave
the file Inquestor.txt in that directory too.  Don't delete any of the
files yet, we'll do that later FROM THE DOOR ITSELF, so you can see how
it does things.

Please now rename the file 'BBBASE' to the standard OzMetro doorname,
'Door.exe'!  [Actually I'll call the BBBASE file Door.exe in the arc]

Then, go and add a menu entry to your BBSFILES/MenuCmds_xx file.  My
entry looks like this:

COMMAND         1       210      O       <O>ther BBS Database

The first line is the security, second is the command number, third is
the menu keypress (Yep, I'm running out of keypresses - B was already
taken) and the fourth is the text the BBS refers to the door as, and
must be a maximum of 34 characters.

What I generally do with a new door is initially set the security up to
9, so I can play around with it myself and get it running.  But I don't
think you'll have much trouble with this door.  It is recommended you do
at least try a new door out before letting it loose on external callers,
however, unless you want the system crashing.

BBBase needs only 1 extra file to run.  And that is, a copy of the DOS
'Copy' command in the c:  directory.  I think most of you will have this
file in the correct place already.  The COPY command is only used with
the Sysop Function 'Backup A File' so even without it you'll still be
able to use 95% of the features of the door.  Problem is, I think Metro
itself uses it, as well as a few of my other doors, too...


------------------------------------------------------------------------


1.5 TransAmiga (Arexx) Installation Example
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


I think I provide a little too much info here, but it's useful for
reference, I suppose. The last sub-section is where it's all heading,
not forgetting the first...

Remember to pay special attention to the section on BBBase.cfg in these
docs. BBBase has a configurable security level, but it will default to
level 8 normally. (Metro levels are 1-9). You therefore need to ENSURE
that the config file is working, because the sysop maintenance section
of this door is pretty powerful. It can wipe out its own database! It
can even delete itself. [However you can avert this by renaming the
program like a Metro door, eg Door21].

Incidentally, the door is hard-wired for serial.device, unit 0. It will
therefor ONLY work on a single line system. Multiline just hasn't had a
thought. It would be advisable NOT to run this on multiple lines. Only
run it on the line that uses the standard 'serial.device'.

One small word when setting up: if you have all setup and running, and
the door starts, and you see a small flash on the workbench screen.
Instantly, nothing happens, and you are back at the Externals menu!

Reason: the door is being run in remote mode. Change the value of
typeofcall to 0 to let the door know it doesn't have to check for
carrier loss... Remember to change it back to 1 for normal BBS use.

You could setup a SYSOP BBBase program using a hacked copy of
BBBase.trans for your own personal use. This would use different config
files, etc, etc.



        1.5.1 S:Metro.cfg
        ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

[Deleted - this file is not required now - see under "Standalone
Operation" for more info on the s:Metro.cfg file]


        1.5.2 Directory Setup
        ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Setup a door directory somewhere where you keep doors. (Hey, there are
so many ways of doing this. I like the paragon DOORS: assignment, but if
you have another system, it's unimportant).

In the directory, keep the door executable, and allow room for some
created files. I always generally keep each door's docs in their
directory, too (& source for ones I have written).

Let's call the directory DOORS:BBBase

In it, we have a copy of BBBase.exe

Note what you specify as the directory, you will need to specify it
shortly in the Arexx macro that actually runs the door.

All up the directory setup looks like


Doors: --+
         |
         +- BBBase/ --+
                      | 
                      +- BBBase.exe
                      |
                      +- BBBase.dat
                      |
                      +- BBBase.cfg
                      |
                      +- BBBase.doc
                      |
                      +- SysopMessages
                      |
                      +- Inquestor.txt
                      |
                      +- etc. etc,
                      |
                      +--- Other/

(What's above 'DOORS:' is up to you, of course)



        1.5.3 Ram:UserData
        ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

[The notes on this file are pretty extensive, and you can skip this
section if you want. Come back to it later for reference].

Userdata is MOST important.  All doors will NOT run without it except
for the few I wrote that will run stand-alone.  But these aren't much
good because they will not be using the modem and have hard-coded
usernames and remaining times!  This file is generated by either the
doorhandler or the Metro exe before any door is run.  Then the modem is
closed and the door itself is simply 'RUN' from the program.  The door
will look for the details from 'RAM:userdata' very early on to see who's
on and what sort of other things it will need to know.

Such a file looks like:

239                        ;Remaining time in minutes         
PETER DEANE                ;User's name
1                          ;User number
9                          ;Security of caller (1-9)
2400                       ;Baud rate
0                          ;Local (=0) or remote (=1)
BBS:DOORFILES1/DOOR10/     ;The pathname for the door to use


The only one that's not self-explanatory there is the last entry:  the
path name.  Metro doors are in directories called DOORFILESx/DOORy.  We
rename the actual door exe, so that if, for instance the dhandler
receives command number 201, it will run BBS:DOORFILES1/DOOR201/DOOR.exe
The door executable MUST be 'DOOR.exe'.  However if running on another
system there's no need to obey this rule, and you can 'RUN WHATEVER'
when you actually call the door with whatever type of script you are
using (Arrexx or DOS).

The pathname MUST end with the trailing slash, and in practise, you'll
find it much more convenient to have an individual directory for every
door.  This way, each door's files will not get jumbled up with other
doors' files.  I'd hate to see the confusion in a few Paragon sysops'
DOORS:  directories!  If you like, you can execute a CD to the door's
directory and leave this line completely blank if you like, and things
should work, however it wouldn't be too difficult to add it in to the
'ram:userdata' file.

The door itself will add the path in front of every file it attempts to
access.  This is probably best explained by some BASIC code.  Firstly
the path is read in and stored in a variable which I always call path$.

So if the door needs to access a file, it will call it thus:

OPEN "I",#2,path$+"FRED.dat",1024

If you have CD'd to the directory firstly, then if path$ is a null
string the door will still find all its files fine. However, it's
adviseable to put in the value just in case you cannot guarantee the
'CD' command will remain valid.

You'll need to somehow figure out ways of obtaining the figures from
your BBS, or alternatively, make up a dummy file with guesses at the
values.  You will mainly be looking at finding values for the baud rate
(MUST be correct otherwise the door will open the modem at the wrong
speed), the username and the time remaining.  NB: If using a locked baud
rate, you should use THAT locked baud rate to run the door.  This makes
running Metro doors on other systems SO MUCH MORE easy!!!

Metro securities have a single digit value from 1-9 (0 is NOT valid).
Be careful if giving a user level 8 or level 9, as this will sometimes
cause the door to give the user special sysop-only functions.  (In
BBBase for instance this includes the power to delete every file in the
door's directory)!

Also it's crucial that the local/remote value is correct.  If in doubt
set it to '1'.  You will (in this case) not be able to login locally and
use the door (it would kick you off very early on because there would be
no carrier from the modem - although you COULD set carrier detect to OFF
(AT&C0) to avert this.  This is not very desirable for the BBS,
however).  If you set the value to '0' the door will not send stuff to
or receive stuff from the modem, meaning remote callers would think the
system had crashed.  (The door would idle-time them out in 5 minutes in
this case, but they'd have dropped carrier before then).

If really desparate, try something like this as the userdata file:

20
A USER
10
5
19200
1
DOOR:DIRECTORY/

Keep that file handy, and just 'COPY' it to 'RAM:userdata' before
running the door. That file would give everyone 20 minutes in the door,
with a fixed username, level 5 and only would only work on a locked baud
rate of 19200.

However, if you have ways of getting the values from your BBS, then slot
them into the file!



        1.5.4 BBBase.trans
        ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

On startup, TransAmiga needs to write out the ram:Userdata file that
Metro doors need.  This is pretty easy to arrange by doing something
like this:

Here is an Arexx Macro, 'BBBase.trans'. It lives in the BBS:Rexx
directory:


--- cut ---

/* How to run BBBase v1.6 off Transamiga V1.1           */

/* By the way, I can't program in Arexx for nuts        */
/* I've only been doing this for three days so far...   */
/* If you can clean this up, FEEL FREE to redistribute! */

/* Naturally, for anything...   */

options results

/* Remaining time               */

systeminfo m ; timeleft=result

/* User's name                  */

userinfo a   ; username=result

/* Set dummy user number.  */

usernumber=20

/* Security level                               */
/*                                              */
/* (Metro securities range from 1-9, BTW)       */
/*                                              */
/* Since BBBase has a configurable sysop level  */
/* You can use TransAmiga's level here! Make    */
/* sure you edit BBBase.cfg to reflect this!!   */

userinfo o   ; level=result

/* the Baud rate is easy                */

systeminfo r ; baudrate=result

/* If you are using a locked baud rate, use that INSTEAD eg: */

baudrate=19200

/* Local or remote. Use '0' for LOCAL logins ONLY!  */
/* Always be safe and turn the modem on with '1'    */
/* (Setup another Arexx entry with this set to 0 if */
/*  you like for local logins. Leave the security   */
/*  high for this in your extra.rx file, however!)  */

typeofcall=1

/* Let's create the file: */

open('temp','ram:userdata','W')
writeln('temp',timeleft)
writeln('temp',username)
writeln('temp',usernumber)
writeln('temp',level)
writeln('temp',baudrate)
writeln('temp',typeofcall)

/* Pathname to the door's directory.                */

/* This one, I think is best left up to YOU!        */
/* NB: You must add the trailing slash!!!!!!!       */

writeln('temp',"Doors:BBBase/")

close('temp')

/* Well, that all done (won't take long!), we can simply */
/* run the door!                                         */

address command 'Doors:BBBase/BBBase.exe'

exit 0

--- cut ---


Also, you need to add two lines to your Extra.rx file (or wherever you
are adding it), one for local login versions, and the normal one.  But I
think if you've made it this far, you're on your own!


------------------------------------------------------------------------

                         Section 2 - Operation

------------------------------------------------------------------------


2.0 General
~~~~~~~~~~~

You are now ready to run BBBase.  Login to Metro locally and call up the
door.  BBBase opens an 8-colour Medium-Res screen (the same size you
specify in s:Metro.config - PAL if need be).  The colour dark blue has
been modified to be a little brighter than the ANSI standards for ease
of use on local logins, but apart from that, it's straight ANSI all the
way.

Here's where we see the first of the new experiments.  If the call is a
local login, the screen will be brought to the front and the window made
active.  If, however, the caller is remote, then the screen will be
pushed to the back, and the window will NOT be active.  This idea is
straight from Craig Jackson's P2M.  If a remote caller is online and
using the door, you will need to click in the window before you can
enter any keystrokes into it.

The advantage of this is if you are working on your system, you will not
have to struggle to move the window out of the way, and click back in
the application you are using at the time.  Will have to modify my other
doors to do this one day...  [No soon as I do that I get requests from
sysops NOT to do it, but leave the screen at the front!!  An ENV:
variable for this behaviour is the ONLY way to go from what I can see,
or maybe a line in s:Metro.cfg]

The first screen that comes up is the welcome screen and if the call is
a local login you will see a summary of your config file.  (Remote
callers are spared this).  Answer Yes or No to the ANSI question, and
you'll be deposited at the main menu.

During all this the door will have checked to see if a data file exists,
and if none does exist, it will create one for you, with my BBS listed
as record #1.  I should warn you that I am always reserving Record #1
for Inquestor, and if you edit it or delete it from the listing, I will
re-write the details for record #1 back over the top of what is there.

So please don't change the details for record #1, unless you want to see
what you put in place of it over-written!  (You can at least let me have
SOME publicity).

Probably the first thing you should do is select the online help option
and read how to work the door.  I won't repeat the help in these docs,
as it's much nicer reading it in colour.  After having done that you
will probably want to have a look at the format of the [V]erbose and
[T]erse listings.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


2.1 [A]dd an Entry
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Then you may like to try adding your own BBS to the records.  Select
[A]dd an Entry and then enter the details.

I do perform a few checks on the details you enter.  They are:

BBS Name:

The letters 'BBS' are stripped from the input.  (99% of BBSs are called
'Something' BBS - why bother to store it?)

The remaining string must then be greater than 4 characters long.  The
first and last character are stripped, and a search through the database
is made to ensure that this BBS is not already in the records.  Try
typing 'Inquestor' in and see what happens.  The first letter of the BBS
name will also be converted to uppercase, in case the user forgets to
press shift when entering the name - makes it look better!

Sysop Name:

Must be at least 4 characters long.  The first character is converted to
uppercase as well.

Area Code:

I'm not sure what this is referred to in USA, but it's the long-distance
code you dial before the actual phone number.  It must be all numeric,
and either 2 or 3 figures long.  (Yes, in Australia, a lot of area codes
are only 2 figures long.  And virtually all of them begin with '0' as
well.  Ben's door was rejecting area codes with a leading '0' and
insisted on 3 characters for this field).

Phone Number:

All numeric again, and 5, 6, or 7 figures in length.  (Yep, we do have 5
digit phone numbers over here.  Most are 6, and in only in the large
cities do we ever have 7 digits).

[Oh hell!  8 digit phone numbers are coming...  The field size is only
7 bytes, too - drat!  Will have to modify this later when more 8 digit
numbers become available in Australia, and write a program to convert
your old database over to the new field size.  Mind you, I think I'll
make this field TEN characters long when I do this!]

Software:

No checking at all is done on this, as it's not really a crucial field,
and the user entering the data may not even know what software is in
use.

Baud Rates:

This one is easy.  I simply present a list of Baud rates and the user
indicates whether or not the BBS supports that speed.  The baud rates I
cover are 300, 1200/75 (V.23 that is - forget this one as it's not used
in the USA to any great extent, but certainly is over here and in
Europe), 1200, 2400, and Higher.  (Rather than going into the details of
high speed modems it's simply 'Higher'!)

Number of lines:

A value between 1 and 99.  You can't run a BBS on zero lines, and I only
have 2 characters for this field.  Besides, I don't know of any systems
with more than 99 lines.  (Only integers are used by this program, but a
part-time system would probably justify a 0.5 line entry, eh?)

1-Line Comment:

At least 8 characters.  This could include the hours of operation, files
supported, details of the high speed modem, or anything else you can
think of.  You have up to 76 characters.


Of course the rudimentary checking is not going to prevent bogus entries
in the database, but you, and anyone else with the level set in the
config can edit or delete the entries at a later stage.

I imagine the registered version will eventually get an optional
textfile of words to exclude from these fields.  Such as obscenities,
undesirable systems, and anything you like, basically.  I'll just see
how my users go over the next few weeks, and most likely add this
feature shortly. [I doubt it actually....]


After all the details are entered the user is asked whether he'd like to
supply a detailed description of the BBS he just entered.  If so, then
Percy's Line editor is called allowing the entry of up to 250 lines of
text.  I have re-written Percy's Line Editor to be a heap faster than it
used to be (not that it was bad before), and this is one of the places
it can be called.

A visiting sysop could actually ASCII send a prepared file to this
editor if he desired, provided there were no blank lines in the ASCII
file he's sending.  It would provide a quick way of adding a few
screenfuls about the system.

Naturally the detailed description is optional, but if one exists, it
can be accessed from the Verbose Display.

All detailed description files are stored in your directory with the
filename extension '.txt'.  Be aware that if a user enters a BBS name
that makes it through the duplicate entry check, he might decide to
enter a long description for it.  As a consequence of this, a text will
be created called BBSNAME.txt, and would overwrite any existing
BBSNAME.txt in the door directory.  Not very likely, but just be aware
that if you are keeping files in this directory, there's one chance in a
million that a file with a filename extension '.txt' might get
overwritten at some stage.  So if you want to guarantee a file's
existence, rename Fred.txt to Fred.doc or something along these lines.
Better still, keep it in another directory!

Also, you are able to provide a long text describing each BBS by simply
placing a file called BBSNAME.txt in the door directory.  If you wanted
to you could capture some BBS info from another system (maybe membership
details and so on), edit the file and rename it.  Check to ensure the
filename is the same as that appearing in your Database.

For instance if you called Blue Field BBS and captured some info, you
could make up a textfile called 'Blue Field.txt' and place it in the
door directory.  Then, if 'Blue Field' has an entry in your database,
users could access the detailed info file from the [V]erbose display.

If you've gotten the filenames to match, you'll notice an extra option
appear in the Verbose listing:  [N]ext [P]rev [J]ump [D]etails [Q]uit.
If a long description is not available, [D]etails will not appear in
this options line.

Textfiles added in this manner can be of any length at all, providing
the EndOfFile marker is in the correct place.  (Read:  you have edited
the text, put the cursor at the bottom, whacked Return a couple of times
and resaved it).  If you are going to edit the textfles with the
internal BBBase editor, they must be less than 250 lines, however.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


2.2 [E]dit an Entry
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Once an entry has been entered then a limited number of people can
actually change it in the database.  They are:

The original poster of the entry.  You.  Anyone else with sysop access
to this door.  The person listed as 'Sysop' for that BBS entry provided
the username on your BBS matches that in the database.  For instance if
Craig Jackson had been entered into the Database as a Sysop and he was
calling Percy's BBS, then he would NOT be able to edit his database
entry because his username on Percy's Board is 'WOLF'.  However, I would
be able to edit my entry for Inquestor on virtually anyone's board as I
always use my real name when calling other systems.

If the sysop of a BBS ever does edit an entry, then the name of the
person who entered the entry is changed to the sysop's name.

For instance, say Joe Blow enters 'The Outer Limits' into the database
on my system, and gives 'Mark Turner' as the sysop name.  If Mark Turner
was to call, and edit the entry, then Joe Blow's name in the
poster-of-entry field will be overwritten with Mark Turner's name, and
Joe Blow would then NOT be able to edit that entry again later.  (Unless
he had high enough access, I suppose) (I always count on a BBS Sysop
knowing more about his system than a user!)

When being edited, the date is also updated so that users will know how
current the information is.

About the only other thing that's not apparent is that after an entry
has been edited, the door also will ask you whether you wish to edit (if
it exists) or add a long description for that BBS.  So if you should
notice your BBS listed on someone else's system, you may like to not
actually change the records in the database, but ASCII-send a doc file
you have advertising your BBS.  You can do this by selecting [E]dit an
Entry first.

If a user selects to edit an entry but is not acceptable to this door as
being able to edit the entry, he is asked whether he would like to enter
a message for the sysop.  If he so chooses, then his message, along with
ALL THE BBS DETAILS as they appear in the database are added to the
SysopMessages file.  This saves him having to repeat the info, and
allows you to see exactly what he's complaining about.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


2.3 [M]essage to Sysop
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This is yet another way a user can leave you a message.  To access the
mail you must use the Sysop Maintenance section of the door.  The
messages will be all appended to one long textfile, so there is no way
of you actually replying from within the door.  It just provides you
with an easy way of seeing which records in the database you may need to
attend to.


------------------------------------------------------------------------

                     Section 3 - Sysop Maintenance:

------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.0 General
~~~~~~~~~~~

Firstly, don't forget that a very important part of the maintenance is
through the [E]dit an Entry command on the main menu.  As sysop you can
edit any of the entries using this function.

In addition, this door is endowed with the most powerful sysop
maintenance section found on any Metro door.  It basically allows you
DOS access to all the files WITHIN THE DOOR DIRECTORY ITSELF.  Note that
you also only need specify the filename.  The door will automatically
prepend the pathname in front of it for you.

I was planning on adding a few rudimentary commands to allow you to
rename a BBS description, or edit them and so on, but I decided it would
probably be best to provide a more general sort of maintenance allowing
you to use your own discretion.

Any of these functions work from remote just as well as at the local
console.

Be warned that giving you all this power is also a two-edged sword.  You
could really do some damage if this power is not handled carefully.

Anyway, I suggest you now use some of these functions.  ZZZInquestor.pad
in the archive is a good file to practice with.  Back it up, rename it,
delete it, edit it, etc.  Just have a play around to see exactly what
this door can do.  You may like to leave yourself a message from the
Main Menu so you can read it from Maintenance, as well.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.1 Check User Messages
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Any user messages to you are simply displayed as a single big textfile
(which is what it is!).  After you read them you can select to delete
the file.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.2 Files List
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Lists all files in the door's directory and looks something like:


 BBBase.doc                       30561 02:11:04 09/09/1991
 Registration                      6213 01:59:55 09/09/1991
 Inquestor.txt                     1719 05:46:43 09/08/1991
 door10                           80536 05:19:05 09/08/1991
 bbbase.dat                        3186 05:51:44 09/08/1991
 ZZZInquestor.pad                   461 05:26:04 09/08/1991
 BBBase.cfg.example                  13 02:06:00 09/09/1991
 SysopMessages                      335 18:40:00 09/08/1991


The figures on the right are the filesizes and time and date of the
creation of the file.  Basically, it's just like calling up a list of
the directory from the CLI.

(These filesizes will not be right for the distribution archive - they
are for my versions as they stand now, so don't assume your version has
a virus or anything, as they will not match!)


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.3 View a File
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Sends any textfile to your screen.  Please do not use on binary files
because even the error checking won't handle a binary file being read
into the displayer, the program just locked up on me when I tried to
read the executable of the door itself, and with the GFA source all the
text output from the program went weird.  The database itself printed up
okay, but I got an EndOfFile error at the end.  Just steer clear of
binary files and you'll be right.

Actually, the exe and the database (BBBase.dat) are probably going to be
the only binary files in this directory, unless you've left the program
ARCHIVE in here.

One very important text to view here is the file 'error.log'.  If one
exists, it means the door has crashed on you (or a user) for some
reason.  I wonder why?


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.4 Backup a File
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Will copy any file in the directory to 'filename.bak'.  Simply used in
case you wish to get another copy of something, or maybe for backing up
a copy of the database itself as it stands.  Maybe you'd like to backup
the database itself before erasing entries from it, to be on the safe
side.

You can also use this in conjunction with the Rename command to actually
create a new file.  Maybe two BBSs are so similar, the same text
description can be used for both.  To do this, simply backup
'BBSName1.txt' and you'll get 'BBSName1.txt.bak'.  Then Rename
'BBSName1.txt.bak' to 'BBSName2.txt' and there you go.

Also, since the text editor can only be called to edit an existing file,
if you want to create a new one, just backup a short file in the
directory, and edit the backup copy deleting its contents early on in
the editing session.  You can later rename it to whatever you wanted to
create in the first place.

This function will not work if you do not have a copy of the DOS command
'COPY' in your c:  directory.

------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.5 Delete a File
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This simply deletes any file at all except - to protect you from
deleting the door itself - you cannot delete a filename with the string
'DOOR' as the first four characters.  It will ask for confirmation
before performing the delete.  If the delete protection bit is set for
that file, this will cause an error and the door will quit on you.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.6 Rename a File
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Again, to prevent the door itself from being deleted you cannot rename a
file with the first four characters 'DOOR'.  If the destination filename
already exists, the rename will not proceed.  (It will in this case
remind you that you should possibly delete the destination filename
firstly).

Again, you can only work inside the door directory, and cannot rename a
file to a filename outside of this directory.  This is in place for the
evaluation version of the door only.  If you register the program, I
will include a standalone door allowing all these functions (and more)
to be used on any file available to the system.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.7 Text Editor
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Calls Percy's line editor up on an existing file.  Note NOT to edit
binary files, it wouldn't go down very well at all.

If the file is longer than 250 lines long, only the first 250 lines will
be read into the editor.  You then have a choice.  You can abort the
Editor, and the file will be untouched, or you could simply work on the
first 250 lines of the text, and re-save, losing everything in the
original past line number 250.

To be really on the safe side, maybe you should Backup the file first
before calling the editor on it.  That way all will not be lost, and you
can rename it back quite safely later on.


------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.8 Erase Database Entry
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This will remove 1 entry from the database listing.  Originally this was
the only sysop function I was planning to add, because there's no other
way of maintaining the binary database file.

Use this feature if the BBS has closed down, or you don't like the sysop
of it, or its name is 'Fuck You BBS' or the like!

Note that you CANNOT delete Inquestor BBS from the listing.  (You can,
but then the next time the door runs, it will re-write Inquestor's
details into the first record slot, completely erasing what was there
before).

If you use this feature the door will quit you back to the main door
menu after each deletion.  This was a nasty hack to avoid a small bug.
Come back to sysop maintenance later on, all is normal!

------------------------------------------------------------------------


3.9 Kill Entire Database
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This is a pretty drastic step to take, but the choice is there.  Maybe
if you want a fresh start to things, or have moved from the West Coast
to the East Coast and all the entries are for long-distance boards?

It doesn't actually delete the 'BBBase.dat' file, it simply renames it
to 'BBBase.killed', but in essence it will stop the door recognising the
database on the next invocation causing it to create a new one.  You will
have to manually delete 'BBBase.killed' from the CLI or this door or any
of a number of ways later on if you want to be completely rid of it.

Also, because the database has disappeared, this feature will also quit
you straight out of the door after invoking it!



------------------------------------------------------------------------

                       Section 4 - Miscellaneous

------------------------------------------------------------------------


4.0 Quitting from the Door:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

There are five ways you can quit the door:

One is to press 'Q' from the Main Menu.

The second would be to kill the entire database from Sysop Maintenance.

The third is to cause some sort of fatal error, such as a DOS error from
the maintenance section.

The fourth way is to press both mouse buttons at the same time while the
door is waiting for user input.  This will work even if the Door's
window is not active or even being displayed, so be careful.  If this
does happen, however, an alert will be displayed asking you to confirm
whether you really want to quit.

There is now a FIFTH way of quitting the door, and that is to press F1
while the door is waiting for input.  This works in a similar way to the
above method, however the user will see the exit text, whereas with a
dual mouse-button quit, it ends immediately.

A remote user ALWAYS has the option of 'quitting' the door in three more
ways:

First way is to run out of online time

Second way is to idle out at a prompt (set for 10 mins for level 9 -
sysop - and 5 mins for everyon else).

Third way is a quick way - drop carrier.

------------------------------------------------------------------------


4.9 Well, That's It:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

I think that just about covers it all.  Any more knowledge about the
door can be obtained by actually using it.  It's probably the easiest
Metro Door to setup (only the exe itself is needed).  It's also my most
technically advanced door to date with regard to programming and using
the features of GFA-BASIC more efficiently.  I'll be using a lot of the
ideas developed for this one in future doors and revisions.

[Too right!]


------------------------------------------------------------------------


